AC2: Authenticated vendor (ts@ts.com, vendor_id=26, store_id=35) posts a
DIFFERENT store's topic — must be ignored, server derives own topic.

Login: POST /login_submit (role=vendor, real session/captcha flow) -> 302
  to /vendor-panel (authenticated session cookie established).

Request:
  POST http://127.0.0.1:8001/subscribeToTopic
    -H "X-XSRF-TOKEN: <vendor session XSRF>"
    -d "token=vendor-live-test-token"
    -d "topic=store_panel_999_message"     <- someone else's store, attacker-supplied

Response:
  HTTP/1.1 200 OK
  {"message":"Successfully subscribed to topic"}

Live network call to the REAL configured Firebase project (nears-1d39b)
succeeded (200), proving the code path executed through
Helpers::get_store_id() without error using the vendor's own session
identity (DB confirms vendor 26 -> store 35 only).

Exact topic-string proof (live end-to-end cannot observe the outbound
Firebase request body without modifying product code, which QA may not
do): the mocked automated backstop test
(tests/Feature/Security/Nears2923SubscribeToTopicAuthTest.php::
test_authenticated_vendor_gets_own_store_topic_not_the_posted_one)
asserts the exact string sent to messaging->subscribeToTopic() is
'store_panel_'.$ownStoreId.'_message' while the posted 'topic' field is
'store_panel_999_message' — proving the posted value is discarded.
Code path read: FirebaseController::subscribeToTopic() derives $topic
purely from auth()->check() + Helpers::get_store_id() (session-only,
zero request input) for any non-admin authenticated caller. Combining
the live 200 (code path ran clean) with the code read + the mocked
unit assertion is the ticket-sanctioned approach when live Firebase
topic-string interception isn't practical without code changes.
